    "lawCode": "Amd §61, Vol Ffs Ben L",
    "lawSection": "Volunteer Firefighters' Benefit Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4562               Revised 4/19/2021\n\nSPONSOR: BROOKS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the volunteer firefighters' benefit law, in relation to\ndeath or disability due to vascular rupture; and to repeal section 4 of\nchapter 668 of the laws of 1977, amending the volunteer firefighters'\nbenefit law relating to disability due to disease or malfunction of the\nheart or coronary arteries, in relation to making certain provisions\npermanent\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo ensure that vascular rupture claims made under the Volunteer Fire-\nfighter's Benefits Law are covered and paid expeditiously.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: This act shall be known and may be cited as the \"Chief James\nBrooks Jr. Act\".\n\nSection 2: Amends § 61 of the VFBL to create a presumption of coverage\nfor vascular rupture where such rupture results in the death or disabil-\nity of a volunteer firefighter, the disability or death arose from the\nduties and activities of a volunteer firefighter.\n\nSection 3: Extends § 61 of the VFBL permanently. Section 4: Provides an\nimmediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe proposed legislation would ensure that firefighters who suffer a\nvascular rupture causing death or disability related to their duties and\nactivities as a volunteer firefighter are covered under the VFBL.  A\nsignificant example of the need for this legislation is James Brooks, a\nvolunteer firefighter and Second Assistant Fire Chief from Whitehall,\nwho suffered a torn aorta on his way to fight a structural fire in the\nTown of Dresden. James initially survived the incident, but tragically\nsuccumbed to his injury months later. While these types of injuries are\ngenerally eventually covered under the VBFL, and his own company did not\nfight the claim, certain insurers still fight these claims in an attempt\nto argue that they could not be related to duties as a firefighter,\nunnecessarily increasing litigation costs, and delaying the payment of\nqualifying benefits to volunteer firefighters injured in the line of\nduty, while protecting their communities.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:  None\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
